  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nPersistent stratus this afternoon is keeping temperatures on \nthe cool side, only rising so far into the upper 60s to low 70s.\nSites that manage to clear off will warm quickly within a short\ntime, like has happened for Brainerd and the Phillips areas. We\nhave been monitoring a cluster of showers and isolated storms \nwhich moved out of North Dakota this morning, and have been \nslowly decaying as they move east. Between the stratus and a \ngeneral lack of instability or shear, this trend will continue \nthis afternoon. In fact, doubt there will be much more than a \nfew sprinkles for parts of Itasca and northern Cass counties \nbefore these decay completely by late afternoon. What stratus \ndoes not break up this afternoon is likely to expand in coverage\nonce again tonight, with some associated fog. Places that clear\nout this evening are more likely to develop locally dense fog \nbefore the stratus slides in over it. Areas under clouds will \nhave better visibility, as happened this morning. \n\nOur main upper level pattern keeps that ridge axis sitting just\nwest of the forecast area tonight, and with some weak ripples \nmoving through that flow, we may get another cluster of \nshowers/storms to affect the western CWA late tonight into \nSunday. Forcing is rather weak and we are going to have to wait \nand see on details before increasing pops much over what we have\nnow. \n\nSunday and Sunday night presents a bit of a conundrum, as we \nmay have a repeat of today, with stratus lingering over the area\nto keep temperatures on the cool side. However, if we can break\nout of the clouds, it will be warm and humid with highs in the \nmid 70s to low 80s. Scattered showers and thunderstorms will \nspread in from west to east during the daytime into the evening,\nwith some stronger ones possible for north central Minnesota. \nStorms will again weaken and decrease in coverage as they move \neast into the Arrowhead and northwest Wisconsin. \n\nThe active weather pattern continues into next week as a \nstrong, cut off upper level low pressure system crosses the \nnorthern Rockies, pushing a surface low from where it develops \nover eastern Montana early Monday northeast into northern \nManitoba by Tuesday night, continuing northeast through \nWednesday. This system appears almost fall-like with the tightly\nwound surface low and deep upper level low. This system should \nbring us a few rounds of showers and thunderstorms beginning \nMonday afternoon into Monday night, with additional chances for \nprecipitation continuing Tuesday into Wednesday as slow moving \nfrontal features move across the area. It appears the risk for \nsevere storms Monday should stay to our west, but we may get \nsome stronger storms Monday night. We will have the potential \nfor strong to severe storms Tuesday afternoon and evening with \nthe best potential for the overlap of sufficient instability and\nshear. \n\nThe latter half of the work week will become cooler with highs\non Wednesday potentially staying in the 60s to low 70s with\nclouds and a few showers. Blustery westerly winds will develop\nfor Wednesday in the wake of the front passage, with gusts\nreaching 30mph or stronger. Conditions settle down through the\nremainder of the work week, with slowly moderating temperatures\nand a slight chance of lingering showers.",
